//! Two-terminal parts.
//!
//! Library parts state only what is always true of them; a design narrows the
//! rest. Names of symbols and footprints belong to the per-tool export mappings,
//! not here. A `pins` table is the datasheet's pin table: the pads here are the same in
//! every package.

/// A resistor. Its through-current and voltage drop are tied together by Ohm's law, and the
/// power it dissipates, `.drop * .current`, is at most `.max_power`, in the worst case over the
/// ranges of the current and the resistance. A design that does not reach the check (a resistor
/// whose current nothing states) lists it as not evaluated, unless Ohm's law over its ends proves it. A catalog row with no `max_power`
/// is an unrated part and is never picked.
///
/// `.temperature_coefficient` is how far the resistance moves with temperature, as a fraction
/// per kelvin (`0.00005` is 50 ppm/K). A design states it only when it needs a limit
/// (`.r.temperature_coefficient <= 0.0001`); a catalog row that does not state it is then
/// not offered, and a design that does not mention it picks rows as before. The standard
/// catalog has no value for it yet, so a design that limits it needs a project catalog row
/// with a recorded source.
///
/// The power rating (ADR 0025). `.max_power` is a column of the `resistor` catalog, read for all
/// 219 rows from the `Power(Watts)` attribute of the JLCPCB record and cross-checked with the LCSC
/// record, equal for every row. It is not derated for a hot board. The check uses the product
/// `.drop * .current` because `.drop` already is `.current * .resistance` (Ohm's law, a relation
/// of the part), so it carries the tolerance of the resistance and the window of the current, and
/// the product is the largest power they admit; a current that flows the other way has a negative
/// drop and gives the same power. The worst case is the current the circuit really carries:
/// `.led.current &= 5mA..15mA` is a window the LED must stay in, and the equation `.led.current
/// == (.input.voltage - .led.forward_voltage) / .r1.resistance` says what the current is for the
/// resistor under test and narrows the window to that value, so the resistor is checked at about
/// 7 mA to 10.6 mA and 36 mW, not at 15 mA and 88 mW (the narrowing rule is in the guide's
/// "Narrowing and relations", and in `propagate` in `crates/netsmith-solve/src/solve.rs`).
///
/// An unrated resistor. A catalog row with an empty `max_power` is never offered. That is the rule
/// the loader already had for any empty cell (the row has no value there and fits no design), and
/// it is the safe one for a rating: a part that cannot be shown to be within its rating is not
/// picked. "Unchecked with a note" was rejected, because a design that states the current would
/// get an unchecked part without knowing. The solver's "nothing for it" message counts the rows
/// left out for an empty cell. No row of the standard catalog is blank.
///
/// Left open, for the owner: is "never offered" the rule the owner wants for an unrated part, or
/// "offered and not evaluated"?
generic Resistor {
    .designator &= "R"

    pins {
        .a passive "A" "1"
        .b passive "B" "2"
    }

    .resistance: Resistance
    /// The power rating at the ambient temperature the datasheet names, not derated.
    .max_power: Power
    /// The change of resistance per kelvin, as a fraction of the resistance: `0.00005` for
    /// 50 ppm/K. Use the datasheet's largest figure.
    .temperature_coefficient: Number
    /// Through-current, a to b. In a `~>` chain from a rail, the first part's is drawn from the
    /// net the chain starts on (ADR 0014).
    .current: Current
    /// Voltage at a relative to b.
    .drop: Voltage

    .drop == .current * .resistance
    .drop * .current <= .max_power
    bridge .a ~> .b
    dc resistor .a .b .resistance

    .package: Package
    catalog resistor { .resistance, .max_power, .package, .temperature_coefficient optional }
}

/// The dielectric class of a ceramic capacitor, by the code its listing and its datasheet print.
/// A design that needs one states it on the capacitor (`.dielectric &= Dielectric::X7R`); a
/// design that does not leaves it open, and a row of the catalog states it only where the
/// recorded source of the row prints it (`data/jlcpcb/README.md`). A class that is not here
/// is added to this list together with the rows that have it (ADR 0055).
enum Dielectric {
    C0G,
    X5R,
    X7R,
}

/// A ceramic capacitor. At DC it passes no current, so it has no `current` and adds nothing
/// to the sum of currents at a net.
///
/// `.capacitance` is the marked value with its tolerance, as on a bill of materials.
/// `.effective_capacitance` is what the circuit can use: the marked value after the DC
/// voltage across the part has taken its share, and after the tolerance, in the worst case
/// over the range `.bias` can take. A design states what it needs of the second where that
/// matters (a regulator's output capacitor) and of the first where it means a marked part
/// (a bypass capacitor). The solver works the effective value out from the part's published
/// curve and refuses a part that has none, but only when the design asks for it.
///
/// `.bias` is the voltage across the capacitor. A capacitor placed between a rail's `pos`
/// and `neg` takes it from that rail; a design states it only for one that is across no rail.
///
/// `.count` is how many identical capacitors stand in parallel in this place (a bank, ADR
/// 0052). Left unset it is one. A whole number (`.count &= 2`) is that many. A range
/// (`.count &= 1..4`) lets the solver choose: it takes the fewest of the range for which every
/// check that mentions the part holds, together with the catalog row, so that a requirement
/// that no single capacitor meets (`.count * .effective_capacitance >= 47uF`) can be met by
/// several. Every other field is the value of one capacitor of the bank. The netlist, the
/// bill of materials and the schematic have one part for each, with consecutive reference
/// designators.
///
/// `.dielectric` is the class of the ceramic (`Dielectric::X7R`), read from the catalog row
/// and stated by a design only when it needs one. A generic capacitor (no catalog row) has
/// none, so a design that states it is given a row. The package is `.package`, a text such as
/// `"0603"`: a design that needs a size states it (`.package &= "0603"`).
generic Capacitor {
    .designator &= "C"

    pins {
        .a passive "A" "1"
        .b passive "B" "2"
    }

    /// The marked value, with its tolerance.
    .capacitance: Capacitance
    /// The capacitance left at `.bias` and within the tolerance, over the whole range
    /// `.bias` can take.
    .effective_capacitance: Capacitance
    /// The DC voltage across the capacitor, in either direction.
    .bias: Voltage
    .max_voltage: Voltage
    /// How many of this capacitor stand in parallel; unset is one.
    .count: Number
    /// The dielectric class, unset until a design states it or a catalog row does.
    .dielectric: Dielectric
    bridge .a ~> .b

    .package: Package
    catalog capacitor { .capacitance, .effective_capacitance, .max_voltage, .package, .dielectric optional }
}

/// A light-emitting diode. Current flows from anode to cathode.
generic Led {
    .designator &= "D"

    pins {
        .anode   passive "A" "2"
        .cathode passive "K" "1"
    }

    .color:           Color
    .forward_voltage: Voltage
    /// Through-current, anode to cathode. An LED does not conduct the other way, so a supply
    /// that could fall short of the forward voltage (a 3.3 V rail and a 3.4 V part) cannot
    /// drive it.
    .current: Current
    .drop:    Voltage

    .drop == .forward_voltage
    .current >= 0mA
    bridge .anode ~> .cathode

    .package: Package
    catalog led { .color, .forward_voltage, .package }
}

/// An inductor. `.inductance` is the marked value with its tolerance. `.saturation_current`
/// is the current at which the inductance has fallen by the datasheet's stated fraction; a
/// design that drives more than that has less inductance than it counted on, so
/// `.current <= .saturation_current` is checked. `.current` is the largest current through the
/// part, the peak and not the average, when it carries a ripple; in a `~>` chain it is the
/// draw of what is behind the chain (ADR 0014), the average, and a design that knows the ripple
/// adds half of it to the saturation check itself (`BuckStage` does). The winding's resistance
/// drops `.current * .dc_resistance`.
///
/// `.rms_current` is the heating (RMS) rating: the DC current at which the datasheet says the
/// part warms by the rise it states. The rise is the datasheet's own and differs between
/// makers, so a row records it in its notes. Nothing in the library checks it: a design that
/// needs one states `.rms_current >= 2A` itself, and a row with no value there is refused.
generic Inductor {
    .designator &= "L"

    pins {
        .a passive "A" "1"
        .b passive "B" "2"
    }

    .inductance: Inductance
    .saturation_current: Current
    /// The resistance of the winding at DC.
    .dc_resistance: Resistance
    /// The heating (RMS) current, where the catalog row records it.
    .rms_current: Current
    /// Through-current, a to b.
    .current: Current
    /// Voltage at a relative to b.
    .drop: Voltage

    .drop == .current * .dc_resistance
    .current <= .saturation_current
    bridge .a ~> .b
    // At DC an inductor is its winding (ADR 0076, amendment 6): the node past a buck stage's
    // inductor is the switch node less what the load makes the winding drop.
    dc resistor .a .b .dc_resistance

    .package: Package
    catalog inductor { .inductance, .saturation_current, .dc_resistance, .package, .rms_current optional }
}

/// A ferrite bead: a lossy inductor that blocks high frequencies. `.impedance` is the
/// magnitude at `.impedance_frequency`, where the datasheet gives it, and `.rated_current` is
/// the largest DC current. The bead's DC resistance drops `.current * .dc_resistance`.
generic FerriteBead {
    .designator &= "FB"

    pins {
        .a passive "A" "1"
        .b passive "B" "2"
    }

    .impedance: Resistance
    /// The frequency at which `.impedance` is specified.
    .impedance_frequency: Frequency
    .rated_current: Current
    .dc_resistance: Resistance
    /// Through-current, a to b.
    .current: Current
    /// Voltage at a relative to b.
    .drop: Voltage

    .drop == .current * .dc_resistance
    .current <= .rated_current
    bridge .a ~> .b
    dc resistor .a .b .dc_resistance

    .package: Package
    catalog ferrite_bead { .impedance, .impedance_frequency, .rated_current, .dc_resistance, .package }
}

/// A quartz crystal with two terminals. `.frequency` is the nominal frequency and its
/// tolerance together, as a range: a 16 MHz crystal good to 20 ppm is
/// `.frequency &= 16MHz +/- 20ppm`. `.load_capacitance` is the capacitance the datasheet
/// calibrates the frequency for, as seen across the two terminals. The two load capacitors
/// are the design's, and they are not tied to `.load_capacitance`: the stray capacitance of the
/// layout is not a datasheet number. Only two-pad footprints are mapped in `std/kicad.toml`, so
/// the four-pad crystals that are most common (3.2 x 2.5 mm) are not (ADR 0023).
generic Crystal {
    .designator &= "Y"

    pins {
        .a passive "A" "1"
        .b passive "B" "2"
    }

    .frequency: Frequency
    .load_capacitance: Capacitance

    .package: Package
    catalog crystal { .frequency, .load_capacitance, .package }
}

/// A fuse, or a resettable fuse in its hold rating: a thermal link that opens above
/// `.rated_current` and that carries up to it. A design puts it in a `~>` chain and its
/// `.current` is the draw of whatever is behind it (ADR 0014), checked against the rating.
/// `.rated_voltage` is the highest voltage it can interrupt. `.resistance` is the cold
/// resistance, which drops `.current * .resistance`.
///
/// `.supply_peak` is the highest voltage the fuse has to interrupt: the `.peak` of the rail the
/// `~>` chain it is in starts on, which the checker gives it (ADR 0086), so `.supply_peak <=
/// .rated_voltage` is checked with no line on the board. A fuse in no chain from a rail has no
/// `.supply_peak`, and the board states it or the check is listed as not evaluated.
///
/// `.limit_current` is the most current the fuse lets through, which is its rating
/// (conservative: a fuse carries more than its rating for a time before it opens). The checker
/// gives it to the parts behind the fuse in the chain that have a `.supply_fault_current` (a
/// diode), which are rated for it (ADR 0091).
///
/// Why `.limit_current` is a field of its own (ADR 0091): the checker could read any
/// `.rated_current`, but a `Switch` and a `Resistor` have one that limits nothing, so a part
/// that limits says so. A part behind the fuse that has `.supply_fault_current` is given the
/// `.limit_current` of the nearest part in front of it that has one, as the relation
/// `part.supply_fault_current == fuse.limit_current`. A fuse behind the part gives it nothing,
/// and the value needs no rail, so a chain that starts on a signal has it. The nearest and not
/// the smallest: it is never less than the smallest, so it is an upper bound and the check is
/// conservative (the smallest would need a `min` that relations do not have), and it is
/// pessimistic when a larger fuse sits between a small one and the diode. The rating is the
/// current the fuse lets through, though a real fuse carries 1.35 to 2 times it for a time
/// before it opens: that factor is the datasheet's, and the board's to state if it wants the
/// diode rated for it (`.d.max_forward_current >= .fuse.rated_current * 1.5`, which still
/// checks). A catalog column for the trip multiple needs a source for each row and is not
/// built. `Diode` has no power rating in std (`.max_power` is a Zener's), so the forward drop
/// times the fault current is not checked. `Switch` and `Inductor` are not given
/// `.supply_fault_current`; it is one line each when a board needs it (the convention: ADR 0105).
generic Fuse {
    .designator &= "F"

    pins {
        .a passive "A" "1"
        .b passive "B" "2"
    }

    .rated_current: Current
    .rated_voltage: Voltage
    .resistance: Resistance
    /// Through-current, a to b.
    .current: Current
    /// Voltage at a relative to b.
    .drop: Voltage
    /// The highest voltage of the rail the chain starts on, transients included.
    .supply_peak: Voltage
    /// The most current the fuse lets through to what is behind it in the chain, whatever
    /// that is: its rating. A part behind it that has a `.supply_fault_current` is given it
    /// by the checker (ADR 0091), so a diode behind a fuse is rated for what the fuse lets
    /// through with no line on the board.
    .limit_current: Current

    .drop == .current * .resistance
    .current <= .rated_current
    .supply_peak <= .rated_voltage
    .limit_current == .rated_current
    bridge .a ~> .b
    dc resistor .a .b .resistance

    .package: Package
    catalog fuse { .rated_current, .rated_voltage, .resistance, .package }
}

/// A single-pole single-throw switch or push button: it joins its two terminals while it is
/// closed. In a `~>` chain it is taken as closed, and `.current` is checked against
/// `.rated_current`.
part Switch {
    .designator &= "SW"

    pins {
        .a passive "A" "1"
        .b passive "B" "2"
    }

    .rated_current: Current
    .rated_voltage: Voltage
    /// Through-current, a to b, while closed.
    .current: Current

    .current <= .rated_current
    bridge .a ~> .b

    .package: Package
}
